____________________________________________________________________________________________________
FINAL STEADY STATE (no absorbing barrier)
____________________________________________________________________________________________________

J N = R F without a barrier: under continuous formation, after the stabilized population has reached its own
steady state, the yields of every exit, chemical activation and thermal reaction together (GO10 eqs. 7-9).

Not available: T = 250 K, p = 37.48 Torr: Banded Cholesky: non-positive pivot -3.751890902111566e7 in row 177; the matrix is not positive definite. The symmetrized operator must be positive definite; in the final steady state this fails when the thermal rate coefficient is negligible compared with the collision frequency (near-singular J); use the intermediate steady state then.
____________________________________________________________________________________________________
THERMAL RATE COEFFICIENTS OF THE FINAL STEADY STATE (lowest eigenpair of J)
____________________________________________________________________________________________________

k_uni = sum of the specific rate coefficients averaged over the normalized eigenvector of the lowest eigenvalue
lambda_1 of J (Gonzalez-Garcia, Olzmann, PCCP 12, 12290 (2010), text after eq. 12); lambda_1 = k_uni in exact
arithmetic (eq. 12), checked as the sum rule (warning above 1.5e-2). Eigen-solver: InverseIteration. The thermal branching is
k_th(channel)/k_uni: the yields of the thermal decay of the network.

Not available: T = 250 K, p = 37.48 Torr: Inverse iteration: eigenpair 0 did not converge in 5000 iterations. (Cholesky factor of S + sigma I with the safety shift sigma = 5.140291351571781e-3 s-1.) What to do: use a full decomposition, which does not need the factor (EigenSolver::FullDecompositionLapack, `--eigen-solver lapack`, or FullDecomposition); it gives k_uni from the thermal eigenvector.
____________________________________________________________________________________________________
THERMAL FATES OF THE WELLS (final steady state with the Boltzmann distribution of one well as the source)
____________________________________________________________________________________________________

The probability that a molecule thermalized in a well ends in each exit of the network, k_x^T J^-1 f0_w.

Not available: Thermal fate of well 'Int1': T = 250 K, p = 37.48 Torr: Banded Cholesky: non-positive pivot -3.751890902111566e7 in row 177; the matrix is not positive definite. The symmetrized operator must be positive definite; in the final steady state this fails when the thermal rate coefficient is negligible compared with the collision frequency (near-singular J); use the intermediate steady state then.
